ion but keeps T1T2T3 complete Chow. T4 has 1 left in wall, relatively safe.  \n\nPlay T1 \u2014 keeps T2T3T4 nearly chow (need T1 or T5) \u2014 but T2T3T4 chow needs T1 or T5 \u2014 we have T1 in hand, so that\u2019s already T1T2T3 chow, not T2T3T4 chow unless we draw T5. Better to discard T4 or T1?  \n\nPlay B3 \u2014 3 left in wall, not too dangerous, but B3 could pair with something.  \n\nPlay J3 \u2014 safest? Only 1 left, no one can pung it. Honors are often safer discards mid-game.  \n\n**Better efficiency**: Keep T1T2T3 (complete Chow) and T3T4 (needs T2 or T5). We have extra T3 already so T3 is a pair candidate.  \n\nSo: Hand if we discard J3:  \nMelds: [W2W3W4], [W7W8W9], (T1T2T3)  \nLeft: T3(pair), T4, W7, B3. Need one more meld (T4 + ?) and actually already have pair T3, so just need one meld from W7B3T4. That\u2019s easier? Actually T4B3W7 \u2014 need another Chow or pung.  \nIf we keep J3 and discard W7:  \nLeft: T1T2T3T3T4 B3 J3 \u2014 that\u2019s T1T2T3 Chow, T3 pair, T4B3J3 all singles \u2014 worse shape.  \n\nMaybe best: discard J3 now.  \n\n**Scoring potential**: We have no pung, all chows so far, pair not fixed.  \n\n**Safety**: J3 discard is very safe \u2014 only 1 left, nobody can peng.  \n\n**Evaluation**:  \nPromising moves:  \n1. Play J3 \u2014 safe, keeps T1T2T3 Chow possibility and T3 pair.  \n2. Play W7 \u2014 safe-ish, keeps honors for maybe Seat Wind? No, J3 is not our seat wind.  \n3. Play T4 \u2014 slightly more risky (T4 only 1 left, but who needs it? Unclear) but preserves T1T2T3 Chow.  \n\nBest: J3 is dead tile with no future meld possible (only 1 left), so discard it first.  \n\n**Reason**: J3 is an isolated honor with almost zero chance to complete a dragon pung (only 1 left in wall). Discarding it now is safe and doesn\u2019t break any useful sequences in hand. Keeping T1T2T3T4 gives more flexibility to form Chow in T suit.  \n\n**Answer**: Play J3"}
